From 35255a1cf074312f17bb487399f6d34c97ee5540 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 18:56:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Run the gateway's daily checkpoint prune on its own
 thread

The daily checkpoint prune was the last chore of the gateway housekeeping
tick and ran inline on that thread. On a laptop gateway one real
prune (git gc of a multi-GB store) took 9 min 20 s (from the prune marker
to the end-of-prune log line). For that whole time the
housekeeping thread did nothing else:

- no runtime heartbeat landed (it is the thread's first chore), so
  gateway_state.json updated_at aged past the 120 s stale TTL and
  `hermes gateway status` / /api/status read the gateway as wedged;
- the restart-safe cron-delivery drain stopped, with every other chore and
  the between-tick DeliveryQueueWatch: a worker's queued send would have
  waited the full 9 minutes.

Why chain
1. Why did the gateway read as wedged? gateway_state.json was not re-stamped
   for 9 min 20 s.
2. Why not? The heartbeat is a housekeeping chore and that thread was busy.
3. Why busy? The checkpoint prune ran synchronously on it (run_chore).
4. Why does a prune take minutes? Its git gc repacks the whole store; the
   cost grows with the store, not with the tick.
5. Why was that on the tick thread? It was moved off the constructor to the
   tick "last, so every other chore of the tick runs first", which bounds the
   delay of that tick's chores but not of the following ticks'.

Fix
gateway/run_checkpoint_prune.py adds CheckpointPruneWorker: one long-lived
daemon thread per housekeeping loop, named gateway-checkpoint-prune.
- request() is O(1): it sets an Event and, on the first call, starts the
  thread inside contextvars.copy_context(), so the prune resolves the same
  Hermes home and profile scope as the housekeeping thread did.
- The thread waits on the Event with no timeout (no idle wakeups), then runs
  one pass as run_chore(logger, "Checkpoint prune tick", auto_prune_from_config).
  Drain custody is unchanged: one admitted gateway.housekeeping lease per
  pass, held for the whole prune; DrainRefused and failures are logged by
  run_chore and the thread survives.
- Requests during a pass coalesce into at most one follow-up pass, which the
  24 h .last_prune marker turns into an immediate return.
- close() stops it: no pass starts afterwards, a running pass is not
  interrupted, an idle thread exits.

_start_gateway_housekeeping keeps the "Checkpoint prune tick" chore in its
slot (last, every tick) but it now only calls worker.request(); the loop
calls worker.close() in a finally. The heartbeat stays the first chore.

The worker thread is started through process_ownership.start_writer, like
the housekeeping thread it used to run on, so a prune that outlives gateway
cleanup still keeps the runtime lock (954d5e9919) instead of silently
dropping that custody with the move.

Unchanged: tools/checkpoint_maintenance.py (prune logic, marker, size cap,
health check), the CLI path, config keys.

Proof (tests/gateway/test_checkpoint_prune_housekeeping.py)
RED with the source files at base 4aa9d2a887 and these tests kept: 8 failed,
1 passed (the pre-existing one-tick test). GREEN at head: 9 passed.
- T1 blocked prune: 3 ticks finish, heartbeat on ticks [1, 2, 3], cron
  delivery drain on ticks 1-3, prune still blocked. Base: the loop thread is
  still alive at the join timeout.
- T2 single-flight: 3 ticks enter the prune once; after release exactly one
  coalesced follow-up pass.
- T3 custody: admit('gateway.housekeeping') on the prune thread, lease live
  during the prune and closed after; a DrainRefused pass prunes nothing and
  the next request runs.
- T4 a raising prune leaves the worker alive.
- T5 the prune runs on gateway-checkpoint-prune (daemon), never on
  gateway-housekeeping.
- T6 after the loop exits the worker thread exits within 2 s and pending
  requests start no prune; request() after close() starts nothing.
- T7 the prune sees the loop thread's contextvars (home override included).

